(* ::Section:: *)
(*Project Title: Using cellular automata to optimize 3D printer infill*)


(* ::Subsection:: *)
(*The Problem*)


(* ::Text:: *)
(*Procedurally generating 3D printer infills for arbitrary shapes using cellular automata.*)


(* ::Text:: *)
(*Traditional infill structures commonly used in 3D printing applications are often based on human assumptions about the ideal shapes for strength, flexibility, and efficient use of material. They take the form of predefined geometric patterns (gyroid, cubic, etc), which are inherently limited in behavior by their complicated rules. Unlike these algorithms, Cellular Automata (CA) display emerging complexity from simple rules, allowing for natural patterns unattainable with human induced constraints. We propose the use of CA based algorithms for the generation of optimized 3D printer infill. In a 1-dimensional scale, it is known that just 255 elementary rules display a wide range of behaviors (including Turing completeness). In 3-dimensions, there are trillions of possible CA rules, signifying a higher probability that there exists rules which provide an infill to conform to specific constraints like lightness, flexibility, and strength. In the WELP:HS program, we aim to create and test our CA infill on 3D prints using a discretized voxel representation and minimizing the size of the voxel to the resolution of the 3D printer in order to get a smooth surface. *)


(* ::Subsection:: *)
(*Abstract*)


(* ::Text:: *)
(*Traditional infill structures commonly used in 3D printing applications are often based on human assumptions about the ideal shapes for strength, flexibility, and efficient use of material. They take the form of predefined geometric patterns (gyroid, cubic, etc), which are inherently limited in behavior by their complicated rules. Unlike these algorithms, Cellular Automata (CA) display emerging complexity from simple rules, allowing for natural patterns unattainable with human induced constraints. We propose the use of CA based algorithms for the generation of optimized 3D printer infill. In a 1-dimensional scale, it is known that just 255 elementary rules display a wide range of behaviors (including Turing completeness). In 3-dimensions, there are trillions of possible CA rules, signifying a higher probability that there exists rules which provide an infill to conform to specific constraints like lightness, flexibility, and strength. In the WELP:HS program, we aim to create and test our CA infill on 3D prints using a discretized voxel representation and minimizing the size of the voxel to the resolution of the 3D printer in order to get a smooth surface. *)


